7†Copyright Policy】\nL44:     * 【18†Order Article Reprints】\nL45:     * 【19†Article Processing Charges】\nL46:     * 【20†Guide for Subscribers】\nL47:     * 【21†Guide for Conference Organizers】\nL48:     * 【22†Special Issue Guidelines】\nL49:   * 【23†Contact Us】\nL50: Search [Input: Search...]\nL51: #### Contents\nL52: \nL53:   * 【24†Abstract】\nL54: \nL55:   * 【25†References】\nL56: \nL57: ## 【8†Journal of Combinatorial Mathematics and Combinatorial Computing】[Input]\nL58: \nL59:   * 【26†Volume 126】\nL60:   * Pages: 279-289\nL61: \nL62:   * Research article\nL63: # On domination polynomials of some graphs\nL64: \nL65: ^{^{1},^{2}}^{}^{}\nL66: \nL67: ^{1}School of Mathematics and Statistics, Shandong University of Technology, Zibo 255049, China\nL68: \nL69: ^{2}Department of Mathematics, Samarkand International University of Technology, Samarkand 140100, Uzbekistan\nL70: \nL71:   * Received: 22/04/2025\nL72:   * Revised: 20/05/2025\nL73:   * Accepted: 29/05/2025\nL74:   * Published Online: 24/06/2025\nL75: \nL76:   * 【27†DOI: https://doi.org/10.61091/jcmcc126-19†doi.org】\nL77:   * 【28†Download PDF】\nL78:   * 【29†Cite】\nL79:   * Copyright Link\nL80:   * 【30†License†creativecommons.org】\nL81: #### Abstract\nL82: The article investigates the domination polynomial of generalized friendship graphs. The domination polynomial captures the number of dominating sets of each cardinality in a graph and is known to be NP-complete to compute for general graphs. We establish the log-concave and unimodal properties of these polynomials, and determine their peaks. Furthermore, we analyze the distribution of the zeros of aforesaid polynomial and identify their region in the complex plane.\nL83: Several open problems are proposed for future exploration.\nL84: Keywords: domination polynomial, zeros, unimodal, peaks, log-concave\nL85: ### 1. Introduction\nL86: We consider finite, simple, and undirected graphs. A graph is denoted by \\(G=G(V,E)\\) with vertex set \\(V\\) and edge set \\(E\\). The order of \\(G\\) is \\(n = |V|\\), and the size is \\(m = |E|\\). An edge between vertices \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) is denoted by \\(u v\\). The degree of a vertex \\(v_i\\) in \\(G\\), denoted \\(d_{v_i}(G)\\) (or simply \\(d_i\\) when the context is clear), is the number of vertices adjacent to \\(v_i\\).\nL87: A complete graph is denoted by \\(K_{n}\\) and its complement by \\(\\overline{K}_{n}.\\) The union of two graphs \\(G_1 = G_{1}(V_1, E_1)\\) and \\(G_2 =G_{2} (V_2, E_2)\\) is denoted \\(G_1 \\cup G_2\\), and has vertex set \\(V_1 \\cup V_2\\) and edge set \\(E_1 \\cup E_2\\). The join of \\(G_1\\) and \\(G_2\\), denoted \\(G_1 + G_2\\), is the graph with vertex set \\(V_1 \\cup V_2\\) and edge set \\[E_{1} \\cup E_{2} \\cup \\{uv \\mid u \\in V_{1},\\, v \\in V_{2}\\}.\\]\nL88: A nonempty subset \\(S \\subseteq V(G)\\) is called a dominating set of \\(G\\), if every vertex in \\(V(G) \\setminus S\\) is adjacent to at least one vertex in \\(S\\). The minimum cardinality among all dominating sets of \\(G\\) is the domination number of \\(G\\), denoted \\(\\gamma(G)\\). For an overview of domination in graphs, see [【31†14】]. Domination sets are useful in network monitoring, when sensors or guards are stationed to view each node.\nL89: They are also important in social network analysis, which involves simulating the diffusion of influence through key persons.\nL90: Let \\(D(G, k)\\) denote the number of dominating sets of size \\(k\\) in a graph \\(G\\). The domination polynomial of \\(G\\), denoted \\(D(G, x)\\), is defined as \\[D(G, x) = \\sum\\limits_{k = \\gamma(G)}^{n} D(G, k)\\, x^k.\\]\nL91: This polynomial serves as a generating function for the number of dominating sets of each size. A root of \\(D(G, x)=0\\) is called a domination root of \\(G\\). In general, computing the domination polynomial is difficult, as the smallest nonzero term occurs at the domination number \\(\\gamma(G)\\), and determining whether \\(\\gamma(G) \\leq k\\) is NP-complete [【32†11】], where \\(k\\) is a positive integer.\nL92: We note that the degree of the polynomial \\(D(G, x)\\) is order \\(n\\), which is same as \\(|V(G)|.\\) As all coefficients of \\(D(G, x)\\) are positive, then all the real zeros of \\(D(G, x)\\) are non-positive. Furthermore, for every graph \\(G\\), it is clear that \\(0\\) is always root of \\(D(G, x)=0\\). The roots of graph polynomials provide useful information about graph structure. Brown and Tufts [【33†7】] investigated the roots of domination polynomials in several graph families.\nL93: A conjecture suggests that the set of integer domination roots in any graph is a subset of \\(\\{-2, 0\\}\\), (see [【34†3】]). The domination polynomial of the friendship and book graphs were studied in [【35†5】], along with their zeros and their limiting curves. Graphs characterized by their domination polynomials are of particular interest. For instance, complete multipartite graphs are determined by their domination polynomials [【36†6】].\nL94: For further developments on domination polynomials, see [【35†5】, 【37†1】, 【38†4】, 【34†3】]. The other types of graphs like independence, independence domination, matching polynomials can be seen in [【39†15】, 【40†21】, 【41†8】, 【42†9】, 【43†12】, 【44†17】, 【45†2】, 【46†22】, 【47†18】, 【48†16】, 【49†20】, 【50†19】]. Motivated by these properties of \\(D(G, x)\\), we carry forward the study of the domination polynomial of friendship graphs and their generalizations.\nL95: We discuss their log-concave, unimodal properties along with peaks and investigate their zeros.\nL96: ### 2. Domination polynomial friendship graph\nL97: \nL98: A sequence \\(S = \\{s_0, s_1, \\dots, s_n\\}\\) of real numbers is called unimodal if there exists an index \\(t\\) with \\(0 \\leq t \\leq n\\), referred to as the peak (or mode) of \\(S\\), such that \\[s_0 \\leq s_1 \\leq \\dots \\leq s_t \\quad \\text{and} \\quad s_t \\geq s_{t+1} \\geq \\dots \\geq s_n.\\]\nL99: A polynomial \\(p(x) = \\sum\\limits_{i=0}^{n} \\alpha_i x^i\\) is said to be unimodal if the sequence of its coefficients \\(\\Pi = \\{\\alpha_0, \\alpha_1, \\dots, \\alpha_n\\}\\) is unimodal. In particular, if \\(t = 0\\), then \\(\\Pi\\) is decreasing, and if \\(t = n\\), then \\(\\Pi\\) is increasing.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoot geometry of domination polynomials for friendship and book graphs (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.08998v1)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2604.08998v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 515\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL15:   3. 【5†2 Preliminaries and notation】\nL16:   4. 【6†3 Question 3.1: Exact number of real roots of $F_{n}$】\nL17:   5. 【7†4 Question 3.2: Upper bounds on the modulus of the roots of $F_{n}$】\nL18:   6. 【8†5 Question 3.3: What can be said about the domination roots of book graphs?】\nL19:   7. 【9†6 Question 3.6: Integer domination roots】\nL20:   8. 【10†7 Conclusion and future work】\nL21:   9. 【11†References】\nL22: \nL23: 【12†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org】\nL24: \nL25: arXiv:2604.08998v1 [math.CO] 10 Apr 2026\nL26: # Root geometry of domination polynomials for friendship and book graphs\nL27: \nL28: Bilal Ahmad Rather Affiliation: School of Mathematics and Statistics, Shandong University of Technology, Zibo 255049, China Email: bilahamadrr@gmail.com\nL29: ###### Abstract\nL30: This study examines the domination polynomials of friendship graphs and book graphs, focusing on unanswered questions related to these families. For the friendship graph $F_{n}$, with even $n$, we show that the polynomial $D(F_{n},x)$ has exactly three real zeros: $0$ and two simple zeros in the intervals $(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$. We further show that these two nonzero zeros have monotonic variation and converge to $-1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$ and $-1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$, respectively.\nL31: We obtain the quantitative approximation $(|z|-1)^{2}\\log|z|\\leq n$ for any complex zeros of $D(F_{n},x)$, resulting in the explicit bound $|z|\\leq 1+\\sqrt{\\tfrac{n}{\\log 2}}$. For book graphs $B_{n}$, we ascertain the comprehensive limit set of domination roots and establish results about the presence of real roots contingent on parity.\nL32: We provide a partial answer to the integer-root an issue by establishing that friendship and book graphs have no nonzero integer domination roots, whereas for corona families, the only nonzero integer root is $-2$.\nL33: 2020 Mathematics Subject Classification. Primary 05C69; Secondary 05C31, 30C15.\nL34: \nL35: Keywords. domination polynomial; domination root; friendship graph; book graph; limit of zeros.\nL36: ## 1 Introduction\nL37: \nL38: Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph. A subset $S\\subseteq V$ is called a dominating set if every vertex of $V\\setminus S$ has a neighbor in $S$. The domination polynomial of $G$ is the generating function of domination sets, and is defined as\nL39: \nL40:  | $$D(G,x)=\\sum_{i=\\gamma(G)}^{|V|}d(G,i)x^{i},$$  |\nL41: where $d(G,i)$ denotes the number of dominating sets of size $i$ and $\\gamma(G)$ is the domination number of $G$. Domination theory is a well-established component of graph theory, driven by robust structural and algorithmic considerations, encompassing resource allocation, coverage, and network resilience issues; key references are [【13†17】, 【14†5】].\nL42: From an algebraic perspective, domination polynomials encapsulate a significant amount of combinatorial information within a singular entity, and the geometry of their zeros frequently reveals underlying regularity in the examined graph family. This philosophy is already recognized from independence, matching, and chromatic polynomials, where the positioning of zeros has resulted in both structural theorems and asymptotic phenomena [【15†10】, 【16†11】, 【17†12】, 【18†14】, 【19†13】].\nL43: The interest in dominance roots has increased due to the combinatorial richness and computational complexity of dominating sets. Generally, ascertaining whether $\\gamma(G)\\leq k$ is NP-complete [【20†16】], hence precise formulations for $D(G,x)$ are seldom and much sought after. Numerous graph classes are recognized as being defined by their domination polynomial, although others are not; refer to [【21†1】, 【22†6】, 【23†8】, 【24†19】, 【25†23】].\nL44: The interest in dominance roots has increased significantly owing to the combinatorial intricacy and computing complexity of dominating sets. Determining whether $\\gamma(G)\\leq k$ is NP-complete [【20†16】], hence exact formulations for $D(G,x)$ are rare and much desired. Several graph classes are identified by their domination polynomial, whereas others are not, see [【21†1】, 【22†6】, 【23†8】, 【26†22】, 【27†21】].\nL45: Among the most natural triangle-rich families are the friendship graphs $F_{n}=K_{1}+nK_{2}$ and the $n$-book graphs $B_{n}$, obtained by identifying a common edge in $n$ copies of $C_{4}$. The friendship graphs are defined by the Erdős–Rényi–Sós friendship theorem [【28†15】], establishing them as a standard test family for extremal and algebraic inquiries.\nL46: In a seminal paper, Alikhani, Brown, and Jahari [【29†7】] formulated explicit equations for the domination polynomials of $F_{n}$ and $B_{n}$, examined their root configurations, demonstrated a limiting hyperbola for the roots of $F_{n}$, confirmed that $F_{n}$ is not $D$-unique for $n\\geq 3$, and presented multiple unresolved questions regarding precise real-root counts, modulus constraints, book-graph root topology, and integer domination roots.\nL47: The hypothesis presented in [【29†7】] was subsequently advanced in [【30†20】, 【31†3】, 【32†4】].\nL48: This study addresses these unresolved inquiries. Specifically, we reexamine Questions 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, and 3.6 (see Open Problem 【33†2.6】) from [【29†7】]. The initial principal theorem provides a definitive resolution to Question 3.1: when $n$ is even, $D(F_{n},x)$ possesses precisely three real zeros. The demonstration is not a numerical argument; it relies on a rigorous monotonicity analysis of two logarithmic equations derived by moving the variable by $1$.\nL49: We subsequently refine the asymptotic analysis by demonstrating the monotone convergence of the two nonzero real roots to the hyperbola intercepts already indicated in [【29†7】].\nL50: In Question 3.2, we formulate a novel quantitative assessment for all complex zeros of $D(F_{n},x)$, specifically $(|z|-1)^{2}\\log|z|\\leq n,$ which immediately implies the explicit sublinear bound $|z|\\leq 1+\\sqrt{\\frac{n}{\\log 2}}.$ This provides a definitive, verified the upper bound on the modulus, significantly more precise than rudimentary coefficient bounds.\nL51: Our examination of book graphs offers a limited yet thorough response to Question 3.3. Employing the results from [【34†9】, 【17†12】], we ascertain the comprehensive limiting set of domination roots of $B_{n}$ as a union of three algebraic components along with two distinct points. We also establish parity-dependent existence assertions for the real roots of $D(B_{n},x)$ and define the exact intersections with the real axis of the limit set.\nL52: Ultimately, inspired by Question 3.6, we demonstrate that friendship graphs and book graphs lack nonzero integer domination roots. In conjunction with the corona constructions from [【29†7】], this provides a partial affirmative response to the overarching integer-root problem: across the natural families examined, the only nonzero integer domination root that emerges is $-2$.\nL53: The results are intresting in two specific respects. Initially, they resolve a previously unresolved precise enumeration issue concerning the real roots of friendship graphs. Secondly, they transform the qualitative root plots of [【29†7】] into quantitative theorems: precise root counts, monotonic convergence, a sublinear modulus bound, and limit characterization for the zeros of book graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://ijmex.com/index.php/ijmex/article/download/419/283)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ijmex.com/index.php/ijmex/article/download/419/283\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 539\nL0@P0: Journal of Mathematical Extension\nL1@P0: Vol. 10, No. 3, (2016), 35-45\nL2@P0: ISSN: 1735-8299\nL3@P0: URL: http://www.ijmex.com\nL4@P0: Domination Polynomial of Generalized\nL5@P0: Book Graphs\nL6@P0: S. Jahari\nL7@P0: Yazd University\nL8@P0: S. Alikhani∗\nL9@P0: Yazd University\nL10@P0: Abstract. Let G be a simple graph of order n. The domination poly\u0002nomial of G is the polynomial D(G, x) = n\nL11@P0: i=0 d(G, i)xi\nL12@P0: , where d(G, i)\nL13@P0: is the number of dominating sets of G of size i. Let n be any positive\nL14@P0: integer and Bn be the n-book graphs, formed by joining n copies of\nL15@P0: the cycle graph C4 with a common edge. In this paper, we study the\nL16@P0: domination polynomials of some generalized book graphs. In particular\nL17@P0: we examine the domination roots of these families, and find the limiting\nL18@P0: curve for the roots.\nL19@P0: AMS Subject Classification: 05C60\nL20@P0: Keywords and Phrases: Domination polynomial, generalized book\nL21@P0: graphs, family\nL22@P0: 1. Introduction\nL23@P0: Let G = (V, E) be a simple graph. For any vertex v ∈ V (G), the open\nL24@P0: neighborhood of v is the set N(v) = {u ∈ V (G)|{u, v} ∈ E(G)} and\nL25@P0: the closed neighborhood of v is the se