 be\nL66@P1: the family of dominating sets of graph G with cardinality i and let\nL67@P1: d(G, i) = |D(G, i)|. The domination polynomial D(G, x) of G is defined\nL68@P1: as D(G, x) = |V (G)|\nL69@P1: i=γ(G) d(G, i)xi\nL70@P1: , where γ(G) is the domination number\nL71@P1: of G (see [1, 8]). This graph polynomial was introduced in the paper [5]\nL72@P1: that appeared in 2014 but numerous other papers on the polynomial\nL73@P1: appeared earlier. For some very recent developments on the polynomial\nL74@P1: see for example [4, 6].\nL75@P1: A root of D(G, x) is called a domination root of G. The set of distinct\nL76@P1: roots of D(G, x) is denoted by Z(D(G, x)). Calculating the domination\nL77@P1: polynomial of a graph G is difficult in general, as the smallest power\nL78@P1: of a non-zero term is the domination number γ(G) of the graph, and\nL79@P1: determining whether γ(G)  k is known to be NP-complete [9]. But for\nL80@P1: certain classes of graphs, we can find a closed form expression for the\nL81@P1: domination polynomial.\nL82@P1: In the next section, we consider generalized book graphs and compute\nL83@P1: their domination polynomials. Also we explore the nature and location of\nL84@P1: roots of domination polynomial of generalized book graphs in Section 3.\nL85@P1: 2. Domination Polynomial of Generalized Book\nL86@P1: Graphs\nL87@P1: A book graph Bn, is defined as follows V (Bn) = {u1, u2} ∪ {vi, wi : 1 \nL88@P1: i  n} and E(Bn) = {u1u2} ∪ {u1vi, u2wi, viwi : 1  i  n}. We\nL89@P1: consider the generalized book graph Bn,m with vertex and edge sets\nL90@P1: by V (Bn,m) = {ui : 1  i  m − 2} ∪ {vi, wi : 1  i  n} and\nL91@P1: E(Bn,m) = {uiui+1 : 1  i  m − 3} ∪ {uiwj : 1  j  n, i =\nL92@P1: m − 2} ∪ {u1vi : 1  i  n} ∪ {viwi : 1  i  n} (see Figure 1).\nL93@P1: We need some preliminaries to study the domination polynomial of some\nL94@P1: generalized book graphs. The vertex contraction G/u of a graph G by\nL95@P1: a vertex u is the operation under which